## 4. Layer 1: Hard Constraints

A candidate is eligible only if all required conditions pass:

$$
\operatorname{Eligible}(i,v)
=
A_{i,v}
\land
\neg E_{i,f}
\land
R_{i,v}
\land
G_{i,v}
$$

where:

- $A_{i,v}$ = availability indicator, equal to $1$ if candidate $i$ has an open slot overlapping the visit window, else $0$
- $E_{i,f}$ = exclusion indicator, equal to $1$ if candidate $i$ is on family $f$'s exclusion list, else $0$
- $R_{i,v}$ = role/certification match indicator, equal to $1$ if candidate $i$ meets the required certification for visit type $v$, else $0$
- $G_{i,v}$ = logistics feasibility indicator, equal to $1$ if the candidate can satisfy transport or vehicle requirements, else $0$

Only candidates satisfying

$$
\operatorname{Eligible}(i,v)=\text{True}
$$

may proceed to the weighted-scoring stage.

### Example: Twins Case (5034/5035)

| Candidate | Available | Excluded | Certified | Van OK | Eligible |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Emma | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | No |
| Kali | Yes | No | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| Axie | No | No | Yes | Yes | No |

Only Kali remains after Layer 1, consistent with the real scheduling outcome where the family comfort issue was resolved and Kali was retained for both twin visits.

---

## 5. Layer 2: Weighted Composite Score

For each eligible candidate $i$ and visit $v$, compute:

$$
S_{i,v}
=
w_1 R_{i,v} + w_2 F_{i,f} + w_3 D_{i,v} + w_4 L_i + w_5 C_{i,f}
$$

with

$$
\sum_{k=1}^{5} w_k = 1, \qquad w_k \ge 0
$$

The selected candidate is

$$
\hat{i}_v = \arg\max_{i \in \mathcal{E}_v} S_{i,v}
$$

where $\mathcal{E}_v$ is the set of eligible candidates for visit $v$.

### 5.1 Recency Score

This rewards candidates whose last assignment is furthest in the past, mirroring the CSBS logic of reducing assignment repetition.

$$
R_{i,v} = \frac{\min(\Delta t_i, T_{\text{cap}})}{T_{\text{cap}}}
$$

where $T_{\text{cap}}$ is a normalization cap, such as $90$ days.

### 5.2 Family-History Score

This term captures prior interaction with the family and can be oriented toward novelty or continuity depending on the family flag.

$$
F_{i,f}=\sigma_f \cdot \frac{1}{1+n_{i,f}}
$$

with:

- $\sigma_f = -1$ for novelty preference
- $\sigma_f = +1$ for continuity preference

Interpretation:

- If $\sigma_f=-1$, more prior visits lower the score.
- If $\sigma_f=+1$, more prior visits increase the score.

### 5.3 Travel / Distance Score

This penalizes heavier travel burden by comparing the candidate's distance to the largest eligible distance.

$$
D_{i,v} = 1 - \frac{d_{i,v}}{d_{\max}}
$$

where $d_{\max} = \max_{j \in \mathcal{E}_v} d_{j,v}$.

### 5.4 Workload-Balance Score

This favors candidates with lower assignment volume during the current period.

$$
L_i = 1 - \frac{v_i^{(\text{period})}}{v_{\max}^{(\text{period})}}
$$

where $v_{\max}^{(\text{period})} = \max_{j \in \mathcal{C}} v_j^{(\text{period})}$.

### 5.5 Continuity Bonus

This adds a modest bonus when a candidate was assigned to the immediately preceding checkpoint visit for the same family.

$$
C_{i,f} =
\begin{cases}
\gamma, & \text{if } i \text{ was assigned to the immediately preceding checkpoint for } f, \\
0, & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
$$

with $\gamma$ a small constant, such as $0.05$.

