## 6. Worked Numerical Example

Assume the visit is family 5035 at the 12-month checkpoint, with eligible candidates $\{\text{Kali}, \text{Sam}\}$ and weights:

$$
(w_1,w_2,w_3,w_4,w_5)=(0.30,0.25,0.20,0.15,0.10)
$$

| Metric | Kali | Sam |
|---|---:|---:|
| $\Delta t$ (days) | 5 | 20 |
| $R_{i,v}$ | $0.056$ | $0.222$ |
| $n_{i,f}$ | 1 | 0 |
| $\sigma_f$ | $+1$ | $+1$ |
| $F_{i,f}$ | $0.500$ | $1.000$ |
| $d_{i,v}$ (miles) | 8 | 22 |
| $D_{i,v}$ | $0.636$ | $0.000$ |
| $v_i^{(\text{period})}$ | 6 | 3 |
| $L_i$ | $0.000$ | $0.500$ |
| prior checkpoint match | Yes | No |
| $C_{i,f}$ | $0.050$ | $0.000$ |

Then:

$$
S_{\text{Kali},v}
=0.30(0.056)+0.25(0.500)+0.20(0.636)+0.15(0.000)+0.10(0.050)
=0.274
$$

$$
S_{\text{Sam},v}
=0.30(0.222)+0.25(1.000)+0.20(0.000)+0.15(0.500)+0.10(0.000)
=0.392
$$

Thus, in this hypothetical example, Sam ranks higher overall because the workload and travel burden dominate despite Kali's continuity advantage. This illustrates why the composite score must combine all five terms concurrently, rather than relying on continuity alone.

In the real Slack example, the family comfort preference was treated as a stronger behavioral override; in the formal model, that would be captured by a high positive continuity flag $\sigma_f$ or by a hard family-preference override in Layer 1.

