## 7. Layer 3: Tie-Breaking Logic

If the top two candidates are effectively tied, the system resolves ambiguity using family-history information.

$$
|S_{i,v}-S_{j,v}| < \epsilon
$$

Then the winner is selected by:

$$
\text{Winner} =
\begin{cases}
\arg\min_{i} n_{i,f}, & \sigma_f = -1, \\
\arg\max_{i} n_{i,f}, & \sigma_f = +1.
\end{cases}
$$

This means:

- when novelty is preferred, the system favors the candidate with the least prior interaction with the family;
- when continuity is preferred, the system favors the candidate with the most prior interaction with the family.

A practical implementation should also flag a low-margin case when the score difference is small but not strictly tied, because these assignments often require human review.

---

## 8. Sensitivity and Escalation Rules

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A[Composite score computed] --> B{Family has dual-relationship flag?}
    B -- Yes --> C[Auto-exclude flagged candidate<br/>no scoring allowed]
    B -- No --> D{PI hold flag on family record?}
    D -- Yes --> E[Pause auto-assignment<br/>route to PI for manual decision]
    D -- No --> F[Proceed with scoring<br/>and auto-confirm]
```

This mirrors the real workflow in which a family relationship concern required explicit human intervention before the visit could be finalized.

