## 10. Implementation Notes

- Weights $w_1$ through $w_5$ should be stored in a versioned configuration table so the PI or lab manager can modify the policy without changing code.
- Layer 1 filters are non-negotiable and cannot be overridden by a composite score.
- The scoring model is intentionally interpretable and linear, making it easy to explain the recommendation to coordinators and the PI.
- A confidence-margin alert is recommended: when the top two candidate scores are within $\epsilon$, flag the assignment for coordinator review even if it is not a strict tie.

A concise summary of the end-to-end decision rule is:

$$
\text{Assignment}(v) =
\begin{cases}
\text{manual escalation}, & \text{if } \mathcal{E}_v = \varnothing, \\
\arg\max_{i \in \mathcal{E}_v} S_{i,v}, & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
$$

This makes the architecture explicit: no assignment is generated without a feasible candidate pool, and no feasible candidate is chosen without a transparent, auditable score. 